 "asked_at": "2026-10-18T00:45+08:00",
 "answer": "Whether your kids should skip school in Kuala Lumpur depends on official air quality readings and your children's personal health, but **parents have the option to keep them home.**\n\nAccording to the Malaysian Ministry of Education's National Haze Action Plan guidelines:\n\n* **Official School Closures:** Schools are mandatorily closed only when the official **Air Pollutant Index (API)** exceeds **200**. (Note: Check Malaysia's official Department of Environment / APIMS readings rather than third-party apps like IQAir, as calculation methods differ). \n* **Outdoor Activities:** If the API is above **100**, all outdoor classroom activities must be suspended.\n* **Parental Discretion:** The Ministry of Education explicitly allows parents to keep their children at home if they are worried that the haze will negatively impact their health. To do this, you simply need to **notify the school administration beforehand**. \n\n**Consider the following when making your decision:**\n* **Health Status:** If your children have pre-existing respiratory conditions (like asthma), allergies, or sensitive lungs, keeping them home or ensuring they wear a proper mask (such as an N95) during their commute is strongly advised. \n* **Current Local API:** Look up the latest official Malaysian API reading for your specific area in KL. If it is climbing or near unhealthy thresholds, opting to keep them home for safety is fully permitted by the ministry.",
